\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The Jaffe-Janssens criterion and its multiplication}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Jaffe and Janssens work in a finite complex Majorana
algebra whose self-adjoint generators satisfy
#{c_ic_j+c_jc_i=2\delta_{ij}}. The generator index
involution is fixed-point free, and its two exchanged
halves partition all generators. Their reflection is
antilinear and multiplicative. Their normalized trace
#{\tau} gives the Boltzmann functional
#{a\mapsto\tau(a\ue^{-H})}, and division by
#{Z_H=\tau(\ue^{-H})} gives the Gibbs functional when
#{Z_H\ne0}. See \citet{pp. 2-3, (1.4)-(1.6); p. 5, (1.9)}{jaffejanssens2015characterization}.}

\p{Their global gauge invariance means evenness under
#{c_i\mapsto-c_i}. It is distinct from a local lattice
gauge action. Their half-product uses one fixed
#{\zeta\in\{\ui,-\ui\}} on an odd-odd product; its extension
to the full algebra is nonassociative
\citet{Sections 1.2 and 1.5, Definition 1.2, pp. 5-6}{jaffejanssens2015characterization}.}

\p{As a cited input, Theorem 4.2(a) says that for an even,
reflection-invariant #{H}, positivity of the cross-plane
coupling block suffices for reflection positivity of the
unnormalized functional at the fixed coupling. The block
excludes the empty monomial on both sides. Theorem 4.4
adds #{Z_H\ne0} for the normalized conclusion and then
obtains #{Z_H>0}. Self-adjointness of #{H} is not a general
hypothesis of these results. The converse in Theorem 4.2(b)
requires positivity for every inverse temperature in a
neighborhood of zero; Theorem 1.1 summarizes an
all-positive-temperature equivalence. A single positive
form does not give that converse
\citet{Theorems 1.1, 4.2 and 4.4; Remark 4.5, pp. 3, 19-21}{jaffejanssens2015characterization}.}

\p{For the [cellular paired reflection](fdip-00XQ), the
ordinary exterior product, order reversal and fixed
boundary-edge cells differ from those hypotheses.
\ref{fdip-00Y3} rules out the immediate odd-generator
algebra substitution and shows why evenness alone does
not remove the reversal sign. A symbol correspondence
would have to identify multiplication, the functional,
normalization, reflection and positive half. The source's
operator-exponential argument also uses a series and an
approximation \citet{(3.13)-(3.14), p. 16; proof of Theorem 4.2, pp. 19-20}{jaffejanssens2015characterization}.
No such correspondence with the finite exterior weight
has been established here.}
